; The signature-vs-body check compares only representations that
; cannot be punned, because `Int` is this language's universal heap
; handle: `mkSpan` answers a `Span` through an `(-> Int Int Int)`
; signature, `jobPoolNew` a `JobPool` through an `Int`, and comparing
; declared against inferred with `tyCompat` reports 21 files that are
; all doing that on purpose. `tyIsReprScalar` names the exceptions.
;
; It named `Bool` and `Float`. `String` belonged there too and was
; left out for a reason that expired: it used to be the other side of
; the `Int`/`String` fiat, so naming it would have reported every
; handle in the tree. The fiat was deleted from `tyCompat` on
; 2026-08-15 and the corpus retyped honestly, which is what made this
; affordable on 2026-08-17.
;
; It is not a naming difference. A `String` is a header address and an
; `Int` is a number, so a declared `String` over an integer body is a
; null dereference the moment anyone reads it:
;
;   (:: f (-> Int String))  (fn (f x) 42)
;   (strLen (f 1))   ->  `check` OK, exit 139, no output
;
; the `Float`-over-`Int` case one step louder, not a different one.
;
; Measured cost of naming it: 145 diagnostics across 117 of 261 files,
; every one of them inherited through an import from THREE
; declarations. Two were simply wrong - `diag.ax`'s `sevSigil`
; answered `"E"`/`"W"` through a declared `Int`, `codegen.ax`'s
; `importHelpText` built a String through one - and were corrected.
; The third is `stdlib/Str.ax`'s `strWrapOwned`, where a `Str` is BORN
; out of three raw words; it says `(cast String s)` now, which is the
; one site in the tree entitled to say it.

; Refused: the signature promises a header address, the body answers a
; number.
(:: labelOf (-> Int String))
(fn (labelOf x) 42)

; Refused in the other direction, for the same reason.
(:: countOf (-> String Int))
(fn (countOf s) "nine")

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) 0)
